11
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:55,060
Again, if the resurrection didn't happen, then this is just some weird cultural thing that we do.

12
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:59,860
But if the resurrection happened, this is the most important thing that you can consider.

13
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:04,980
And so we'd love to invite you to just consider the claims of Jesus with us this morning.

14
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:12,380
And as we continue, we've been in a series through the book of James, which if you're a skeptic about faith, the Bible, and the claims of Jesus,

15
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,820
the book of James is an incredible apologetic because it was written by Jesus' half-brother.

16
00:01:17,820 --> 00:01:25,500
And if you have siblings, it's hard enough to get your siblings to like you, much less to claim that you're the Messiah sent from God.

17
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:33,240
And so that's a pretty incredible claim that the brother of Jesus would be willing to literally put himself in harm's way,

18
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:37,520
become a martyr for the sake of claiming that his brother was Messiah.

19
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:38,760
Incredible story.

20
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:40,700
We'll get back to that book next week.

21
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:45,340
But I'm going to take a break this week, and we're going to kind of have a resurrection-focused message.

22
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:51,420
So if you have your Bibles and you want to follow along, we're going to be in Mark chapter 8 most of our time this morning.

23
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:53,400
Mark chapter 8.

24
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:59,940
And if you have phones and you want to grab your Bible app, I'll be in the New Living Translation.

25
00:01:59,940 --> 00:02:02,940
It'll also be on the screen behind me.

26
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:04,060
I'm excited.

27
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:06,900
This is my first ever Easter Sunday message.

28
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:09,700
You know, yeah, I'm pumped.

29
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:10,820
I'm pumped.

30
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:15,380
I grew up, I was a youth pastor early on in my days.

31
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:16,840
And then an executive.

32
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:19,020
And I call myself a JV pastor.

33
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:23,480
We get to preach on holidays like Memorial Day and Fourth of July when no one's here.

34
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:28,620
So now that you guys are all here, I'm like looking, and I'm like, people are here and like excited.

35
00:02:28,620 --> 00:02:31,480
So I'm pumped to be sharing with you this morning.

36
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:34,540
Let me pray, and then we'll kind of hop into our text in Mark chapter 8.

37
00:02:34,540 --> 00:02:39,860
God, we are so thankful for the empty tomb.

38
00:02:41,220 --> 00:02:48,660
Like I said, if it didn't happen, then this is just some weird thing that we all kind of are wasting our times doing.

39
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:50,100
But we believe that it happened.

40
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:54,240
And because it happened, this is the most important hour of our days.

41
00:02:57,060 --> 00:03:04,140
God, you have come to give us forgiveness, to give us redemption, victory over sin, death, and the grave.

42
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:06,840
And so, God, just speak to us this morning.

43
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:08,980
Remind us of your goodness and your faithfulness.

44
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:17,900
The reality is there's not a person on earth that is a good enough communicator that can have a message for every single person in this room.

45
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:20,060
But the Holy Spirit is good enough to do that.

46
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:25,640
When Peter gave his first sermon and you say that you translated his message into the language of the hearers,

47
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:31,200
I pray this morning, God, that you would translate the message that you have given me into the ears of each individual in this room,

48
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:32,580
that they would hear a word from you.

49
00:03:32,580 --> 00:03:34,340
Not from me, but from you.

50
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:36,900
God, teach us your word this morning.

51
00:03:38,220 --> 00:03:41,860
Remind us of the victory that we've been invited to walk into.

52
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:48,300
Don't let us settle for something less than resurrection life this morning.

53
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:50,120
In Jesus' name I pray.

54
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:51,100
Amen.

55
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:53,900
So, Mark chapter 8.

56
00:03:53,900 --> 00:03:55,720
We're going to start in verse 22.

57
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:59,060
We'll be all over the chapter this morning, but let's just read with me for a second.

58
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:00,560
Mark chapter 8, verse 22.

59
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:05,780
When they, being Jesus and the disciples, arrived at Beth's day,

60
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:09,460
that some people brought a blind man to Jesus,

61
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:13,380
and they begged Jesus to touch the man and heal him.

62
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:18,280
Jesus took the blind man by the hand, and he led him outside of the village.

63
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:22,120
And then, spitting on the man's eyes, he laid his hands on him,

64
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:22,980
and he asked him,

65
00:04:22,980 --> 00:04:25,520
Can you see anything now?

66
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:28,540
The man looked around, and he said,

67
00:04:28,540 --> 00:04:32,880
Yeah, I see people, but I can't see them very clearly.

68
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:34,820
They look like trees walking around.

69
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:38,460
And then, Jesus placed his hands on the man's eyes again,

70
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:42,660
and his eyes were open, and his sight was completely restored,

71
00:04:42,660 --> 00:04:44,700
and he could see everything clearly.

72
00:04:45,740 --> 00:04:47,100
Jesus sent him away, saying,

73
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:50,300
Don't go back into the village on your way home.

74
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:53,060
This is the word of the Lord.

75
00:04:53,060 --> 00:04:57,000
So, you might be thinking to yourself right now,

76
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,300
It's Easter Sunday.

77
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:04,440
Why in the world would this guy be preaching about Jesus' one and only botched miracle, right?

78
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:08,220
Like, nothing would restore my confidence in the belief and the faith

79
00:05:08,220 --> 00:05:10,160
that Jesus actually rose from the dead,

80
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:13,560
like reading a miracle where Jesus didn't get it right,

81
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:18,820
where he didn't have the power to heal this blind man on the first time through.

82
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:19,160
Why?

83
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:23,940
I knew I should have gone to the Presbyterian church on Easter Sunday.

84
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:28,460
This guy is preaching about Jesus' botched miracle on Resurrection Sunday.

85
00:05:28,460 --> 00:05:29,380
Why this story?

86
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:30,580
Why today?

87
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:33,200
Well, we're going to get there in just a minute,

88
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:36,580
but if you trust me, I'd ask you to close your eyes.

89
00:05:36,580 --> 00:05:37,880
And if you're new here and you're like,

90
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:41,140
Sorry, dude, I don't trust anybody that wears a shirt that looks like that,

91
00:05:41,140 --> 00:05:42,280
I get it.

92
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:44,140
So, you can keep your eyes open,

93
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:49,180
but I'd love for you to just imagine that one or two teachers that you had

94
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:50,680
in elementary, middle, or high school.

95
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:56,200
You know the teachers that, like, brought the subject to life for you?

96
00:05:56,200 --> 00:05:58,340
Teachers that you, like, actually learned from.

97
00:05:58,340 --> 00:06:03,660
They took the boring subject in the book and they brought it to life out of the paper.

98
00:06:03,660 --> 00:06:07,780
You know those teachers that made a real significant impact in your life.

99
00:06:07,780 --> 00:06:12,240
I'm hoping that you're bringing to memory one or two teachers that really taught you some

100
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:13,460
incredible things.

101
00:06:13,460 --> 00:06:18,440
And the thing about really great teachers is oftentimes you learn lessons that they were

102
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:20,680
trying to teach you long after you leave their classroom.

103
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:26,040
Like, they try to get you to understand things and you just weren't ready for it or you just

104
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:27,120
didn't get it in the moment.

105
00:06:27,220 --> 00:06:30,180
But later in life, something happens and a light bulb goes off.

106
00:06:30,180 --> 00:06:34,480
And you're like, oh, now I see what they were trying to do.

107
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:36,840
Now I understand what they were trying to teach me.

108
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:40,280
Those of you that are newer parents, you've probably had these moments, right?

109
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:43,900
Where you're like, you didn't understand what your parents were doing when they raised you

110
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:47,420
and then you had kids and you were like, you had that light bulb moment, right?

111
00:06:47,420 --> 00:06:50,480
Like, oh, I get it now.

112
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:56,460
And the reason that I want to focus our attention on Christ's botched miracle on Easter Sunday

113
00:06:56,460 --> 00:06:59,960
is because the reality is it's not a botched miracle at all.

114
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:02,640
It's a lived out parable.

115
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:08,160
It's a brilliant teaching by Jesus where he was trying to get his disciples to understand

116
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:09,980
that they were missing the point.

117
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:16,700
He was trying to get his disciples to understand that they were confused about who he is and about

118
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:19,980
what he actually came to do.

119
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:25,420
And if you read the Gospels at all, if you read Mark, Mark kind of seems on the surface

120
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:28,840
like it's a rushed journaling of an excited disciple.

121
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:32,800
Like he just sat down and everything that he heard about Jesus and everything that he saw,

122
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:34,080
he just wrote down really quickly.

123
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:43,220
But the reality is the book of Mark is an incredible, deep masterclass on literary communication.

124
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:48,260
Every story in the book of Mark was put where it is on purpose and for a reason.

125
00:07:48,260 --> 00:07:50,680
Mark had two very clear objectives.

126
00:07:50,680 --> 00:08:00,680
And our story, this botched miracle happens to be the hinge point between the two overarching points of the Gospel of Mark.

127
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:07,760
See, the first eight chapters of the Gospel of Mark, Mark is trying to teach that the king has arrived and his name is Jesus.

128
00:08:07,760 --> 00:08:14,560
The first eight chapters cover two and a half years of Jesus' life in ministry where he's literally just building his resume.

129
00:08:14,900 --> 00:08:15,900
He's doing miracles.

130
00:08:15,900 --> 00:08:16,900
He's healing the blind.

131
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:18,180
He's healing the sick.

132
00:08:18,180 --> 00:08:19,360
He's passing out demons.

133
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:23,020
He's telling people, listen, I am the Messiah that has come.

134
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:32,500
Because he knows how radical of a claim that is, you'll see in the book of Mark all throughout the first eight chapters, he'll heal someone and he'll say, listen, don't tell anybody.

135
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:34,980
Don't tell anybody what I've done to you.

136
00:08:34,980 --> 00:08:35,340
Go away.

137
00:08:35,340 --> 00:08:35,800
Go away.

138
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:37,560
And he'll tell the demons.

139
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:39,020
He'll say, get out of this person.

140
00:08:39,020 --> 00:08:42,660
And he'll tell the demons, don't tell anybody who I am.

141
00:08:42,700 --> 00:08:55,220
Because the reality is, for Jesus to claim that he was the Messiah, the Messiah of Israel, not only was it going to be heresy to all of the religious leaders, but it was going to be a threat to the king of Rome.

142
00:08:55,220 --> 00:08:59,700
So he would have been making every single person in power mad.

143
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:06,080
He knew that for him to claim to be the Messiah, that death and persecution was coming.

144
00:09:06,080 --> 00:09:14,160
And so the first two and a half years of his life, he's just doing things as low-key as he possibly can, just to try and get people to see, listen, I am the king.

145
00:09:14,160 --> 00:09:17,740
The king has arrived, and his name is Jesus.

146
00:09:17,740 --> 00:09:32,040
And the second half of the book of Mark literally just covers the last six months of Jesus' life, where he starts to clearly tell them, I am the Messiah, but I'm a different kind of Messiah than you thought.

147
00:09:32,840 --> 00:09:43,200
The last six months, Jesus is on his way to Jerusalem, knowing that he's going to the cross, knowing that he's going to be crucified for the sins of the world.

148
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:46,320
But that's not the type of Messiah that people were expecting.

149
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:53,680
And so the first half, Jesus is saying, I'm this king who has come, who has promised from the Old Testament, but I'm not the type of king that you think I am.

150
00:09:53,680 --> 00:10:00,020
And this miracle that Jesus does is the hinge point between these two things.

151
00:10:00,480 --> 00:10:12,880
And the whole reason he does it, he's a masterful teacher, the greatest teacher who ever lived, he did it to try and get his disciples to realize that they were seeing God's plans with a blurred vision.

152
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:17,240
I think we can learn the same lesson from this box miracle this morning.

153
00:10:17,240 --> 00:10:22,420
Oftentimes, God's people often see God's plans with a blurred vision.

154
00:10:23,120 --> 00:10:26,420
We find ourselves in the same spot that the disciples were in.

155
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:31,460
We grow up hearing these claims that Jesus is king, but he's not the king that he thought he was.

156
00:10:31,460 --> 00:10:40,940
And we try and follow him, but so often we get distracted by the things of this world and we see God's plans, not clearly, but with a blurred vision.

157
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:43,400
This is what the disciples were struggling with.

158
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:49,460
And this is why Jesus used this miracle as a teaching to try and get the disciples to understand that they were missing it.

159
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:52,700
Look what happens right before this miracle.

160
00:10:52,700 --> 00:11:00,840
At the beginning of chapter 8, if you have heading titles, you'll see that Jesus fed 4,000 people with seven loaves of bread.

161
00:11:00,840 --> 00:11:04,060
One of the most incredible miracles that he had ever accomplished.

162
00:11:04,060 --> 00:11:06,280
The disciples were there experiencing it.

163
00:11:06,280 --> 00:11:09,000
They were literally handing out the bread as the bread was multiplying.

164
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,880
They saw God's power, that he truly was the king, the Son of God.

165
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:22,640
And right after they left that miracle, it says this in verse 14,

166
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:25,040
But the disciples had forgotten to bring any food.

167
00:11:25,040 --> 00:11:27,320
They had only one loaf of bread with them.

168
00:11:27,320 --> 00:11:30,200
And as they were crossing the lake, Jesus warned them,

169
00:11:30,200 --> 00:11:35,300
Watch out, beware of the yeasts of the Pharisees and of Herod.

170
00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:41,180
At this they began to argue with each other because they hadn't brought any bread.

171
00:11:41,880 --> 00:11:44,040
Jesus knew what they were saying, so he said,

172
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:47,620
Why are you arguing about having bread?

173
00:11:47,620 --> 00:11:50,620
Don't you know or understand yet?

174
00:11:50,620 --> 00:11:53,580
Are your hearts too hard to take it in?

175
00:11:53,580 --> 00:11:54,620
You have eyes.

176
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:55,400
Can't you see?

177
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:56,280
You have ears.

178
00:11:56,280 --> 00:11:57,200
Can't you hear?

179
00:11:57,200 --> 00:11:59,260
Don't you remember anything at all?

180
00:11:59,340 --> 00:12:05,500
When I fed the 5,000 with five loaves of bread, how many baskets were left over afterwards?

181
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:07,420
Twelve, they said.

182
00:12:07,420 --> 00:12:13,080
And when I fed the 4,000 with seven loaves, how many large baskets were left over?

183
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:15,080
Seven, they said.

184
00:12:15,080 --> 00:12:20,120
Jesus said, Don't you understand yet?

185
00:12:21,500 --> 00:12:28,120
See, the disciples just saw Jesus create enough food for 4,000 people.

186
00:12:28,120 --> 00:12:33,000
And he's trying to teach them a little lesson about how the Pharisees are distorting the message of Jesus.

187
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,960
And they say, Dang, we should have packed more food.

188
00:12:35,960 --> 00:12:38,740
We should have packed a bigger lunch.

189
00:12:38,740 --> 00:12:40,360
And Jesus responds, You have eyes.

190
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:41,020
Can't you see?

191
00:12:41,020 --> 00:12:42,780
Don't you understand who I am?

192
00:12:42,980 --> 00:12:44,100
I am the king.

193
00:12:44,100 --> 00:12:51,000
We've had two and a half years of you seeing me do miracle after miracle after miracle, and you still don't get it.

194
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:55,860
You're seeing God's plans partially, but you're seeing them with a blurred vision.

195
00:12:55,860 --> 00:13:01,300
Can't you see that it doesn't matter how much you packed for dinner or how prepared you are for every situation?

196
00:13:01,300 --> 00:13:03,200
Because I am Messiah.

197
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:04,400
I am king.

198
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:06,700
I can provide everything that you need.

199
00:13:06,700 --> 00:13:11,400
The disciples, they were seeing God's plan, but they were seeing it with a blurred vision.

200
00:13:12,980 --> 00:13:15,160
And do we fall into the same trap?

201
00:13:15,160 --> 00:13:24,360
It's so easy for us to forget the ways in which God has worked in our past, to get distracted by our present trials and temptations.

202
00:13:24,360 --> 00:13:34,360
We lose sight of God's ultimate plan for our life, which is to love the Lord our God with all of our heart, soul, mind, and strength, and to love our neighbor as ourselves.

203
00:13:34,360 --> 00:13:36,560
We see God's plans with blurred vision.

204
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:41,920
We want King Jesus as long as his plans line up with our plans.

205
00:13:42,780 --> 00:13:47,540
As long as his vision for our life lines up with our vision for our life.

206
00:13:47,540 --> 00:13:49,920
As long as his agendas matches our agendas.

207
00:13:49,920 --> 00:13:54,140
As long as he would spend time doing the things that we would spend time doing.

208
00:13:54,140 --> 00:13:58,340
Or, like the disciples, we think that God needs us to accomplish his plans.

209
00:13:58,340 --> 00:14:00,580
The disciples are like, we're not going to eat now.

210
00:14:00,580 --> 00:14:03,380
We're not going to be able to eat because we didn't pack food.

211
00:14:03,380 --> 00:14:05,240
And Jesus is saying, guys, don't you get it?

212
00:14:05,240 --> 00:14:06,200
Like, I don't need your help.

213
00:14:06,200 --> 00:14:08,580
I can do this thing without you.

214
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:12,780
I'm inviting you to participate because I want you to follow me.

215
00:14:12,780 --> 00:14:14,880
Not because I need you to follow me.

216
00:14:14,880 --> 00:14:20,080
And so they go from this teaching directly into Jesus running into this blind man.

217
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:22,400
And because Jesus is the greatest teacher on earth.

218
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:24,360
He's like, I'm going to use this.

219
00:14:24,360 --> 00:14:28,820
Use this as an opportunity to try and get my disciples to see that they're missing the point.

220
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:34,220
That they're seeing my plans with blurred vision.

221
00:14:34,220 --> 00:14:41,120
Imagine what the disciples might have thought when they take this man outside of the village.

222
00:14:41,120 --> 00:14:43,360
It's just the disciples, Jesus, and this blind man.

223
00:14:43,360 --> 00:14:45,500
They've seen him do it a thousand times.

224
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:48,060
Healing the blind, the sick, casting out demons.

225
00:14:48,060 --> 00:14:50,900
He's done it over and over and over again.

226
00:14:50,900 --> 00:14:52,020
They know what's coming.

227
00:14:52,020 --> 00:14:53,540
There's no surprise.

228
00:14:53,540 --> 00:14:55,460
There's no, like, tension here.

229
00:14:55,460 --> 00:14:57,500
The disciples are like, yeah, the blind man's going to get healed.

230
00:14:57,580 --> 00:14:58,140
This is awesome.

231
00:14:58,140 --> 00:14:58,740
We love this.

232
00:14:58,740 --> 00:14:59,440
This is why we're here.

233
00:14:59,440 --> 00:15:00,900
This is why we're following this guy.

234
00:15:00,900 --> 00:15:05,200
And he says, hey, can you see?

235
00:15:05,200 --> 00:15:06,620
The blind man's like, yeah.

236
00:15:06,620 --> 00:15:10,120
I mean, I can see more than I could see before.

237
00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:11,640
But it's not good.

238
00:15:11,640 --> 00:15:13,420
Like, people look like trees.

239
00:15:13,420 --> 00:15:18,580
And I don't know if you've ever been in a situation with, like, a newer magician.

240
00:15:18,580 --> 00:15:20,920
Any of your friends ever pick up magic as a hobby?

241
00:15:20,920 --> 00:15:22,980
And they, like, give you the card trick.

242
00:15:22,980 --> 00:15:24,500
And they're like, is this your card?

243
00:15:24,500 --> 00:15:25,180
They're pumped.

244
00:15:25,180 --> 00:15:26,440
They know that it's your card.

245
00:15:26,540 --> 00:15:29,800
And you're like, it's not my card.

246
00:15:29,800 --> 00:15:33,600
It's such an uncomfortable, weird feeling, right?

247
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:36,520
And this has to be the feeling that the disciples are feeling.

248
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:37,780
They're like, wait a second.

249
00:15:37,780 --> 00:15:38,880
This has never happened.

250
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:42,680
Why is Jesus not able to do this?

251
00:15:42,680 --> 00:15:43,660
Does he lack faith?

252
00:15:43,660 --> 00:15:45,080
Is he distracted?

253
00:15:45,080 --> 00:15:46,480
Does he have things going on?

254
00:15:46,480 --> 00:15:51,460
The disciples probably felt some serious tension, thinking, wow, how can Jesus not pull this off?

255
00:15:51,460 --> 00:15:54,000
How can he not figure this out?

256
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:59,840
But like I said, this is not about Jesus not being able to heal.

257
00:15:59,840 --> 00:16:01,160
It's a hinge point.

258
00:16:01,160 --> 00:16:07,660
It's to try and get them from, I'm a king, to I'm not the king that you are expecting.

259
00:16:07,660 --> 00:16:14,100
See, he intentionally botches the miracle to help the disciples see that they are viewing his plans with a blurred vision.

260
00:16:14,200 --> 00:16:15,540
They're getting it, but not fully.

261
00:16:15,540 --> 00:16:18,580
They're seeing partially, but not completely.

262
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:24,360
Look at the story that happens directly after it.

263
00:16:24,360 --> 00:16:30,480
Again, this is the hinge point for Jesus' king, but he's not the king that he thought.

264
00:16:30,580 --> 00:16:33,180
So he heals the blind man the second time through.

265
00:16:33,180 --> 00:16:38,940
And it says, Jesus and his disciples left Galilee and went up to the villages near Caesarea Philippi.

266
00:16:38,940 --> 00:16:44,120
As they were walking along, Jesus asked them, who do people say that I am?

267
00:16:44,120 --> 00:16:48,300
Well, they replied, some say that you're John the Baptist resurrected.

268
00:16:48,300 --> 00:16:49,960
Some say that you're Elijah.

269
00:16:49,960 --> 00:16:52,340
And others say that you're one of the prophets.

270
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:56,780
And then he asked them, but who do you say that I am?

271
00:16:56,780 --> 00:17:00,000
Peter said, you are the Messiah.

272
00:17:00,820 --> 00:17:03,700
Jesus warned them not to tell anyone about them.

273
00:17:03,700 --> 00:17:13,740
And then Jesus began to tell them that the Son of Man, the Messiah, he must suffer many terrible things and be rejected by the elders, the leading priests, and the teachings of the religious law.

274
00:17:13,740 --> 00:17:18,480
He would be killed, but three days later, he would raise from the dead.

275
00:17:18,480 --> 00:17:27,180
As he talked about this openly with his disciples, Peter took him aside and began to reprimand him for saying such things.

276
00:17:27,880 --> 00:17:30,220
Jesus turned around and looked at his disciples.

277
00:17:30,220 --> 00:17:34,720
And then Jesus reprimanded Peter saying, get behind me, Satan.

278
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:42,420
You're seeing things merely from a human point of view, not from God's point of view.

279
00:17:43,160 --> 00:17:48,960
See how on the other side of this miracle, he says, are your hearts hardened?

280
00:17:48,960 --> 00:17:50,180
Don't you have eyes?

281
00:17:50,180 --> 00:17:50,800
Can't you see?

282
00:17:50,800 --> 00:17:51,560
Don't you have ears?

283
00:17:51,560 --> 00:17:52,100
Can't you hear?

284
00:17:52,100 --> 00:17:52,840
I'm the king.

285
00:17:52,840 --> 00:17:58,800
And then for the first time ever, somebody claims out loud that Jesus is Messiah.

286
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:01,860
And he says, yeah, you're right, but this is what it's going to mean for me to be Messiah.

287
00:18:02,080 --> 00:18:10,260
What it's going to mean for me to be Messiah is that I must suffer many terrible things and be rejected by the leading priests and the elders and the teachers of the religious law.

288
00:18:10,260 --> 00:18:11,220
And I will be killed.

289
00:18:11,220 --> 00:18:13,780
But three days later, I will raise from the dead.

290
00:18:13,780 --> 00:18:19,360
The first time Jesus ever clearly teaches about his resurrection and the disciples miss it.

291
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:24,300
He clearly says, I am going to raise from the dead.

292
00:18:24,300 --> 00:18:26,180
That is an incredible claim.

293
00:18:26,180 --> 00:18:28,920
Like if somebody said that to you, that would stick out to you.

294
00:18:28,920 --> 00:18:29,800
You would hear that.

295
00:18:29,800 --> 00:18:30,700
You would understand that.

296
00:18:30,700 --> 00:18:32,600
Peter, the disciples, they didn't hear it.

297
00:18:32,600 --> 00:18:33,960
They didn't understand it.

298
00:18:33,960 --> 00:18:37,740
Immediately, Peter pulls Jesus aside and says, listen, this isn't going to work for us.

299
00:18:37,740 --> 00:18:45,480
Peter, the disciples, they were seeing God's plans with blurred vision.

300
00:18:45,480 --> 00:18:49,620
And Peter gets a bad rap, but he was the spokesperson for the other disciples.

301
00:18:49,620 --> 00:18:51,900
He was saying what all of them wanted to say.

302
00:18:51,900 --> 00:18:55,200
Yes, Jesus, this isn't going to work for us.

303
00:18:55,200 --> 00:19:00,240
This isn't what we thought we were signing up for when we initially followed you.

304
00:19:00,240 --> 00:19:05,020
We said yes to Messiah, but we didn't say yes to the suffering servant.

305
00:19:05,020 --> 00:19:09,260
We said yes to a powerful political leader, but not to a crucified Savior.

306
00:19:09,260 --> 00:19:14,480
The disciples were still seeing God's plans with blurred vision.

307
00:19:14,480 --> 00:19:17,040
They wanted Jesus' power without his sacrifice.

308
00:19:17,880 --> 00:19:19,960
They wanted breakthrough without submission.

309
00:19:19,960 --> 00:19:22,120
They wanted resurrection without the cross.

310
00:19:22,120 --> 00:19:28,960
And how often do we make the same mistake?

311
00:19:28,960 --> 00:19:39,180
We want to follow King Jesus on his throne as long as we get to skip crucified Jesus on his cross.

312
00:19:39,880 --> 00:19:43,120
We want abundant life without sacrifice.

313
00:19:43,120 --> 00:19:45,280
We want breakthrough without submission.

314
00:19:45,280 --> 00:19:50,340
We want victory without sacrifice.

315
00:19:50,340 --> 00:19:54,340
What does Jesus say to this?

316
00:19:54,340 --> 00:19:59,820
What is his response to this type of attitude to missing the point?

317
00:20:01,460 --> 00:20:04,820
He says, get behind me, Satan.

318
00:20:04,820 --> 00:20:11,240
I don't know about you, but that's not what I want to hear from the king of the entire universe.

319
00:20:11,240 --> 00:20:15,420
I don't want to hear those words coming out of his mouth, but that's what he says to Peter.

320
00:20:15,420 --> 00:20:21,940
And that's what he says to anyone who misses his invitation, who misses his call.

321
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:30,900
And we don't have time to really get into the ramifications of what Jesus is saying, but it's actually a lot less harsh than it sounds upon first reading.

322
00:20:30,900 --> 00:20:39,340
When he says, get behind me, he's literally re-inviting Peter to change his perception, to change the way that he's thinking about God's plans.

323
00:20:39,340 --> 00:20:42,800
See, Peter was thinking about God's plans from a human perspective.

324
00:20:42,960 --> 00:20:45,680
He just says that in verse 33.

325
00:20:45,680 --> 00:20:49,400
He says, you are seeing things merely from a human point of view.

326
00:20:49,400 --> 00:20:50,500
He says, repent.

327
00:20:50,500 --> 00:20:54,380
Start seeing things the way that God sees them.

328
00:20:54,380 --> 00:20:58,580
Start following God's plans, God's ways, not your own way.

329
00:20:58,580 --> 00:20:59,160
Repent.

330
00:20:59,160 --> 00:20:59,760
Turn.

331
00:20:59,760 --> 00:21:00,780
Change your mind.

332
00:21:00,780 --> 00:21:01,900
Come back behind me.

333
00:21:01,900 --> 00:21:03,460
You've gotten ahead of me.

334
00:21:03,460 --> 00:21:06,980
You've been following me all this way.

335
00:21:06,980 --> 00:21:08,460
You knew who I was.

336
00:21:08,460 --> 00:21:09,700
You knew that I was the Messiah.

337
00:21:09,700 --> 00:21:12,540
You followed me for the reasons that you followed me for.

338
00:21:12,600 --> 00:21:15,840
But now when I'm telling you I'm going to the cross, you're trying to get ahead of me.

339
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:17,160
You're trying to change my plans.

340
00:21:17,160 --> 00:21:19,920
You're trying to do things your way because you don't want to go to the cross.

341
00:21:19,920 --> 00:21:23,820
And how often is that our attitude?

342
00:21:23,820 --> 00:21:27,120
How often are we willing to follow Jesus until things get hard?

343
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:31,940
Until he calls us to sacrificially love and forgive the people that we don't want to love and forgive.

344
00:21:31,940 --> 00:21:42,240
When he calls us to sacrificially father our children and be a good husband, even when we're not getting the rewards that

345
00:21:42,240 --> 00:21:48,480
we think we deserve, how often does he want us to continue to follow his example of sacrificial submissive love?

346
00:21:48,480 --> 00:21:52,680
And how often do we say, yeah, but when do I get my stuff in return?

347
00:21:52,680 --> 00:21:54,640
This is not what I thought I was signing up for.

348
00:21:54,640 --> 00:21:57,460
Sure, I'll follow you as long as it's going to work out for me.

349
00:21:59,460 --> 00:22:02,360
Jesus says, Peter, you were getting it.

350
00:22:02,360 --> 00:22:05,400
You were getting it, but now you're missing it.

351
00:22:05,400 --> 00:22:07,940
You're seeing my plans with blurred vision.

352
00:22:07,940 --> 00:22:08,720
Get behind me.

353
00:22:08,720 --> 00:22:09,180
Repent.

354
00:22:09,180 --> 00:22:11,380
Follow me again.

355
00:22:11,380 --> 00:22:14,360
And this is the invitation that he gives to Peter.

356
00:22:14,360 --> 00:22:16,720
And this is the invitation that he gives to all of us.

357
00:22:16,800 --> 00:22:19,240
I love what John Mark Homer says about repentance.

358
00:22:19,240 --> 00:22:28,180
Repentance means to rethink everything you think you know is true and what you think will lead you to life.

359
00:22:28,180 --> 00:22:40,100
The life that you crave and trust in the teachings and the way of Jesus to lead you into a better life and a better kingdom.

360
00:22:40,860 --> 00:22:43,040
This is the invitation Jesus is giving Peter.

361
00:22:43,040 --> 00:22:45,400
This is the invitation he's giving each of us.

362
00:22:45,400 --> 00:22:46,440
Get behind me again.

363
00:22:46,440 --> 00:22:48,340
Rethink the ways that you're thinking.

364
00:22:48,340 --> 00:22:49,840
You're seeing things with a blurred vision.

365
00:22:49,840 --> 00:22:52,660
You're wanting to do my plans, but your way.

366
00:22:52,660 --> 00:22:53,680
Come behind me.

367
00:22:53,680 --> 00:22:55,140
Follow me to the cross.

368
00:22:55,140 --> 00:22:58,760
This is the invitation that he gives to Peter.

369
00:22:58,760 --> 00:23:02,060
And this is the invitation that he gives to all of us.

370
00:23:02,060 --> 00:23:02,700
He goes on.

371
00:23:02,700 --> 00:23:07,420
He says, then, in verse 34, then calling the crowds to join his disciples, he said,

372
00:23:08,380 --> 00:23:13,140
If any of you wants to be my follower, you must give up your own life.

373
00:23:13,140 --> 00:23:16,260
Take up your cross and follow me.

374
00:23:16,260 --> 00:23:20,800
If you are trying to hang on to your life, you will lose it.

375
00:23:20,800 --> 00:23:26,960
But if you give up your life for my sake and for the sake of the gospel, you will save it.

376
00:23:26,960 --> 00:23:32,160
This is the invitation to repent that he's giving Peter.

377
00:23:32,160 --> 00:23:35,060
This is the invitation to repent that he's giving the crowds.

378
00:23:35,060 --> 00:23:39,860
And this is the invitation to repent that he's giving to each one of us this morning.

379
00:23:39,860 --> 00:23:47,640
If we want to follow Jesus, I mean truly follow Jesus, we must be willing to lay down our lives to humbly submit to Jesus,

380
00:23:47,640 --> 00:23:50,040
to follow Jesus to the cross.

381
00:23:50,040 --> 00:23:52,400
We need to rethink what success looks like.

382
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:55,680
Success is not wealth, power, influence, or status.

383
00:23:55,680 --> 00:24:00,100
Success is humble submission to a crucified and resurrected Savior.

384
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:09,720
Jesus gave this invitation because he knew he could do more with a small number of submitted followers than a large crowd of self-focused followers.

385
00:24:09,720 --> 00:24:14,660
He was weeding out people that didn't actually want to follow him.

386
00:24:14,660 --> 00:24:18,640
He was weeding out the people that were just following him for what they could get out of him.

387
00:24:19,320 --> 00:24:28,200
He knew he could do more with a few fully submitted to him than a crowd full of people that just were there for the miracles.

388
00:24:28,200 --> 00:24:33,280
He'd had two and a half years of people that were just there for the miracles,

389
00:24:33,280 --> 00:24:35,740
crowds following him everywhere he went.

390
00:24:35,740 --> 00:24:42,640
And he knew that his kingdom was going to be built by a small minority of people willing to follow him to the cross.

391
00:24:47,840 --> 00:24:49,720
So what does all of this have to do with Easter?

392
00:24:49,720 --> 00:24:55,580
This is a pretty heavy message for Easter Sunday, the empty tomb, the resurrection.

393
00:24:55,580 --> 00:24:57,280
What does all of this have to do with Easter?

394
00:24:57,280 --> 00:25:03,200
Well, remember, at the beginning of this, I asked you about those teachers who had had a massive impact in your life,

395
00:25:03,200 --> 00:25:07,800
those teachers who oftentimes long after you get out of the classroom, the light bulb goes off.

396
00:25:07,800 --> 00:25:12,400
Well, I think that's what we're seeing in this text.

397
00:25:12,400 --> 00:25:19,780
See, most scholars believe that Peter was the eyewitness, the eyewitness testimony of Mark's gospel.

398
00:25:19,780 --> 00:25:24,260
See, Peter is retelling this story on the other side of the cross in the empty tomb.

399
00:25:24,260 --> 00:25:29,520
In the moment when Jesus performed this box miracle, Peter and his disciples, they didn't get it.

400
00:25:29,520 --> 00:25:30,980
They didn't understand what was happening.

401
00:25:30,980 --> 00:25:31,620
They were confused.

402
00:25:31,620 --> 00:25:35,380
How does Jesus not have the power to heal this blind man?

403
00:25:35,380 --> 00:25:40,700
But now on the other side of the cross in the empty tomb, the light bulb goes off for Peter and he says,

404
00:25:40,700 --> 00:25:44,500
Oh, man, is that guy good at teaching.

405
00:25:44,500 --> 00:25:47,520
It wasn't a box miracle.

406
00:25:47,520 --> 00:25:48,700
It was a lived parable.

407
00:25:48,700 --> 00:25:52,860
He was trying to get us to see that we were seeing his plans with blurred vision,

408
00:25:52,860 --> 00:25:57,900
that we were distracted by our own pride and insecurities and anxieties and worries and fears,

409
00:25:57,900 --> 00:26:00,420
that we wanted God's plans to happen our way.

410
00:26:00,420 --> 00:26:06,920
And he was trying to get us to see that God's plans only work God's ways.

411
00:26:06,920 --> 00:26:14,860
Ultimately, what Jesus is trying to teach us through this passage, and what this has to do with Easter,

412
00:26:14,860 --> 00:26:18,340
is yes, God's people often see God's plans with a blurred vision,

413
00:26:18,340 --> 00:26:22,400
but the cross in the empty tomb brings God's plans into focus.

414
00:26:23,700 --> 00:26:28,140
The cross in the empty tomb is the light bulb moment for Peter where he gets it.

415
00:26:28,140 --> 00:26:32,720
He gets that victory comes through sacrifice.

416
00:26:32,720 --> 00:26:37,520
He gets that the empty tomb comes through the cross.

417
00:26:37,520 --> 00:26:48,040
And this only, again, like, for those of you that are in marketing at all, PR,

418
00:26:48,840 --> 00:26:52,000
this invitation is not a very good invitation by Jesus.

419
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,440
Is he supposed to be the greatest teacher in the world?

420
00:26:54,440 --> 00:26:58,740
And his, like, his main message, all of it comes down to,

421
00:26:58,740 --> 00:27:03,260
if you want to follow me, give up your life and take up your cross.

422
00:27:03,260 --> 00:27:05,880
Like, that is not a great invitation.

423
00:27:05,880 --> 00:27:10,660
It's not a great invitation apart from the empty tomb.

424
00:27:11,740 --> 00:27:16,680
But because he goes before us and shows us what the results are of sacrifice,

425
00:27:16,680 --> 00:27:19,460
shows us what the results are of crucifixion,

426
00:27:19,460 --> 00:27:23,160
shows us what the results are of giving up our life to follow him,

427
00:27:23,160 --> 00:27:26,600
it's a far better invitation.

428
00:27:26,600 --> 00:27:29,420
I love how Rebecca Meyer puts it.

429
00:27:29,420 --> 00:27:29,780
She says,

430
00:27:29,780 --> 00:27:36,000
The invitation to suffer and die in order to find life is only made possible

431
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,720
because Jesus takes on this life of suffering first.

432
00:27:39,520 --> 00:27:44,000
This invitation only means anything to us because Jesus goes first.

433
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:49,200
Apart from Jesus' crucifixion, his life, his death, and the empty tomb,

434
00:27:49,200 --> 00:27:53,620
an invitation to give up your life, to sacrifice everything is not very appealing,

435
00:27:53,620 --> 00:27:56,020
but on the other side of resurrection,

436
00:27:56,020 --> 00:28:03,100
this is the most important invitation that any of you will ever receive.

437
00:28:05,700 --> 00:28:08,580
It's an invitation to suffer and die,

438
00:28:08,580 --> 00:28:11,180
but it's an invitation to receive resurrection.

439
00:28:11,180 --> 00:28:17,140
The disciples, they understood the end result of Messiah.

440
00:28:17,140 --> 00:28:22,780
They knew that Messiah was coming to bring justice to the oppressed,

441
00:28:22,780 --> 00:28:24,500
to set the captives free.

442
00:28:24,500 --> 00:28:29,020
They knew that Jesus was a conquering king over sin, death, and the grave.

443
00:28:29,100 --> 00:28:31,760
They just wanted it to happen without the cross.

444
00:28:31,760 --> 00:28:34,360
They wanted it to happen on their terms.

445
00:28:34,360 --> 00:28:40,760
Obviously, 2,000 years removed, we don't still struggle with this new perspective.

446
00:28:40,760 --> 00:28:48,160
Obviously, we don't want to see Christ's victory and resurrection in our lives without self-sacrifice.

447
00:28:51,380 --> 00:28:56,760
See, Peter, I love that Peter has this humility to share this story with us,

448
00:28:56,760 --> 00:28:59,580
to help us see the things that he missed in the moment.

449
00:28:59,580 --> 00:29:05,240
Because he knew that we would be tempted to see God's plans with a blurred vision,

450
00:29:05,240 --> 00:29:10,020
and he knew that the cross and the empty tomb would bring God's plans back into focus.

451
00:29:10,020 --> 00:29:14,080
See, we have the privilege of living on the other side of the empty tomb.

452
00:29:15,760 --> 00:29:19,680
Let's not be a people who continually try and see the victory of God in our life

453
00:29:19,680 --> 00:29:22,820
without being willing to humbly follow Jesus to the cross.

454
00:29:22,820 --> 00:29:29,320
This morning, I want each and every one of us to rethink,

455
00:29:29,320 --> 00:29:32,040
to reconsider the invitation of Jesus.

456
